{
  "concept": "Gases spread by random molecular motion from regions of high concentration to low until both bulbs are uniformly mixed — and the lighter gas (H₂) spreads faster than the heavier one (O₂).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left spherical glass bulb (pale blue-green fill with lighter inner highlight, thick grey-teal outline)",
    "Right spherical glass bulb, identical styling",
    "Short horizontal neck/tube connecting the two bulbs",
    "Stopcock valve assembly drawn on the neck (barrel, handle/knob on top, stem below) with a visibly closed vs. open plug orientation",
    "White circular dots = H₂ molecules, initially all in the left bulb (~30)",
    "Red circular dots = O₂ molecules, initially all in the right bulb (~30)",
    "Label 'H₂' above the left bulb",
    "Label 'O₂' above the right bulb",
    "Caption text beneath the apparatus that changes with state: 'Stopcock closed' / 'Stopcock open' / 'Some time after stopcock open'",
    "Panel letter label (a) / (b) / (c) beneath the caption",
    "Optional molecule-count readout per bulb (H₂ left/right, O₂ left/right)",
    "Optional mass legend: 'H₂ = 2 g/mol (fast)' in white/blue, 'O₂ = 32 g/mol (slow)' in red",
    "Optional faint trail or speed halo on molecules when mass color-coding is on"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "timeElapsed",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Mixing is continuous, not a jump between snapshots: every intermediate position shows partially overlapping populations, with the white H₂ front having travelled farther than the red O₂ front at the same instant.",
      "label": "Elapsed time after stopcock opens",
      "range": [0, 100, 1],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "playDiffusion",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "Diffusion is driven by ceaseless random molecular motion — molecules do not 'aim' for the empty bulb, yet the net flow is always down the concentration gradient until it vanishes.",
      "label": "Play diffusion",
      "loop": true
    },
    {
      "id": "massView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Comparing the two species side by side shows H₂ populating the far bulb sooner than O₂ populates the near one, because rate of diffusion scales with 1/√(molar mass) — the seed of Graham's law.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Both gases", "Color by molecular mass"],
      "default": "Both gases"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "(a) Stopcock closed",
      "narration": "H₂ fills the left bulb and O₂ the right, each perfectly confined. Two steep concentration gradients exist, but the closed valve forbids any exchange.",
      "state": { "timeElapsed": 0, "massView": "Both gases" }
    },
    {
      "title": "(b) Stopcock open — first moments",
      "narration": "Open the valve and molecules immediately stream through the neck in both directions. Notice the asymmetry: several white H₂ molecules already reach the right bulb while only a few red O₂ molecules have crossed left.",
      "state": { "timeElapsed": 25, "massView": "Both gases" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why H₂ moves ahead",
      "narration": "Switch to the mass view: at the same temperature both gases have the same average kinetic energy, so the 2 g/mol H₂ molecules move far faster than the 32 g/mol O₂ molecules and spread roughly four times as quickly.",
      "state": { "timeElapsed": 45, "massView": "Color by molecular mass" }
    },
    {
      "title": "(c) Approaching equilibrium",
      "narration": "Given enough time, both species are distributed nearly uniformly across both bulbs. The gradients are gone, so net transfer stops — though individual molecules never stop crossing the neck.",
      "state": { "timeElapsed": 100, "massView": "Both gases" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Nothing pushes the gases together — random motion alone erases concentration gradients, and because equal temperature means equal average kinetic energy, the lighter H₂ molecules simply travel faster and mix sooner than the heavier O₂.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single apparatus centered on a white background: two equal-size pale blue-green spheres with soft inner highlights and thick grey-teal outlines, joined by a short horizontal neck carrying a grey-teal stopcock (knob above, stem below; rotate the plug 90° when open). Small white filled circles with faint outlines are H₂; slightly larger bright red filled circles are O₂ — scatter them irregularly, never on a grid. At t=0 all white dots sit left, all red dots sit right, packed fairly densely. As time advances, white dots should cross into the right bulb noticeably faster and in greater number than red dots cross left; by t=100 both colors are evenly intermixed in both bulbs. 'H₂' labels the left bulb, 'O₂' the right, both in dark grey serif-ish text above the spheres. A caption line beneath updates between 'Stopcock closed', 'Stopcock open', and 'Some time after stopcock open', with the panel letter (a)/(b)/(c) below it. In the mass-color view, add a small legend pairing white/blue with '2 g/mol — fast' and red with '32 g/mol — slow', and show per-bulb molecule counts so the learner can watch the numbers equalize at different rates."
}

